WEBVTT

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:01.710
JAMES BROTSOS: Hi, everyone.

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:03.270
Welcome. I'm James Brotsos.

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:07.440
I'm a Product Manager working on
securing code and applications.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.910
Today, I want to
show you something

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:10.770
that I think changes the
game for how developers

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:12.780
and security teams
work together.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:15.600
It's a multi-model
agentic scanning harness.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.610
It's exactly what
it sounds like --

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.740
AI agents that just
don't flag problems,

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.300
but actually help you fix them

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.880
at the speed you're
already shipping code.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:28.110
Now, the title of this session
is "Ship Fast, Stay Secure."

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:29.640
And I chose those
words carefully

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.440
because for most developers,

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.810
those two things have always
felt like a trade-off.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:36.870
Either you can move fast,
or you can be secure.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:39.450
Pick one. What I'm going
to show you today is

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:41.800
that you don't have
to choose anymore.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.210
Here's the reality
we all live in.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.580
Application security
is a team sport.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.070
You've got developers on
one side, security managers

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:52.290
on the other, and
somewhere in the middle,

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:54.240
hopefully they're collaborating.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:55.470
But let's be honest.

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.780
In most organizations,
that collaboration looks

00:00:57.780 --> 00:00:59.220
like a security team
dropping findings

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.460
on developers' desks two
weeks after the code shipped,

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.430
or a developer
ignoring a scan result

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.260
because it didn't have
enough context to act on.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.450
The intent is good.

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.850
The workflow is broken.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.130
What if we could
actually close that gap?

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:17.970
Not by adding more process,
more tools, more dashboards,

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.590
but by embedding
security directly

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:22.770
into places developers
already work: your terminal,

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:25.380
your pull request, your IDE.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.330
That's the bet we've made.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:28.920
So let's get specific.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.390
On the left, the developer's
world: code, dependencies,

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:35.730
infrastructure as
code, pull requests,

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:38.250
security alerts, issue tracking.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:41.730
On the right, the security
team's world: security posture,

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:44.820
running workloads, attack
paths, exploitability,

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.910
business criticality,
recommendations.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:51.510
The magic happens in the
middle, where security lives.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.700
That intersection is
what we've built.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:54.930
Microsoft Defender for Cloud

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:57.000
and GitHub Advanced
Security working together

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.640
so that a vulnerability found in
code can be traced all the way

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.400
to the runtime risk in
your cloud environment.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:03.720
And the other direction, too.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:06.420
A cloud risk can be mapped
directly back to the line

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:10.380
of code and the exact
developer who can fix it.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.470
And now, with AI
agents in the loop,

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:15.420
we're not just
connecting these dots.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:18.150
We're explaining the
findings, suggesting the fix,

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:22.260
and in most cases,
opening the pull request.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:22.890
All right.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:23.970
Enough slides.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:25.920
Let me show you exactly
what this looks like.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.780
Let's jump into the demo.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:28.440
All right.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:29.460
Let's get into it.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:30.990
I'm here in my terminal, sitting

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:34.770
in a repository called
Benchmark Python.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.320
It's an open-source project with
some real vulnerabilities buried

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:40.500
in the code, the kind of thing
traditional static analysis

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.340
scanners would typically give
a clean bill of health to.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:46.680
I'm going to run the
Defender CLI powered

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:49.470
by what we call code name MDASH.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:50.520
Here's what makes it different

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.950
from every other scanner
you've used before.

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:58.290
MDASH doesn't rely on a single
model doing pattern matching.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:01.410
It orchestrates over a
hundred specialized AI agents

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:05.580
across an ensemble of frontier
and distilled models,

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.340
and it runs them through
a five-stage pipeline.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:10.350
It prepares the attack surface.

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:12.780
It scans candidate code paths.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.900
It validates findings by having
agents actually debate whether

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:20.400
the bug is reachable and
exploitable, de-duplicates,

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:21.870
and then proves
the vulnerability

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:24.450
by constructing
triggering inputs.

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:26.070
The model is one input.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:29.130
The system around
it is the product.

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:33.600
Now, that's the same engine that
is running right here in my CLI.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.680
Let me kick off a scan.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:38.820
Look at that.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:39.870
It caught vulnerabilities

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.600
that traditional pattern
matching completely misses.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.350
MDASH understands systematic
logic flow, and it's not looking

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:48.240
for known bad strings.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:51.210
It's reasoning about what
the code actually does,

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:53.310
the way an attacker would.

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:56.310
And all of this happened
right here in my terminal.

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:58.230
No waiting on pipeline.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:01.170
The shift-left started right
here before the code even

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:03.990
touched the repository.

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:08.400
Okay. So now that we've
found some vulnerabilities,

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.950
this is where it gets
really interesting.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:13.770
I just don't want to have
this list of findings.

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:16.890
I can fix them right
here, right now.

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:19.680
So let me issue the fix command.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.380
So, in here, I find
my result file,

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:26.220
which is a standardized
output SARIF file.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:30.880
And I am going to now
submit a fix for it.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:38.940
What's happening right now is

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.640
that Copilot is analyzing
the vulnerability,

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:43.500
understanding the
context of the code,

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.990
and making the actual
change to fix it.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.330
No context switching,
no filing a ticket

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:51.420
for later, no copy, pasting.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:56.220
The AI understands what's broken
and rewrites the code to fix it.

00:04:56.220 --> 00:05:01.560
So this is the developer
loop: scan, find, fix.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.940
All without leaving
your terminal.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.040
So the CLI found the
vulnerabilities,

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:11.220
and Copilot generated fixes,
but I'm not just going

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:12.960
to blindly accept them.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:15.110
So I'm going to switch
over to my VS Code,

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:18.470
where I can actually
see what changed.

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:23.690
In the background, I do have a
scan that ran, or a fix earlier.

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:27.390
And you can see here that it'll
read through all the files,

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:31.710
apply its logic,
edit those files,

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:36.270
and then write the new files
with the fixes itself.

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:39.540
So for these next steps, I'm
going to review the pilots

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:41.700
and then re-run the
test to make sure,

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:46.440
and then I can commit those
changes into my repository.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:48.120
Here's the beauty
of this workflow.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:52.410
I can see exactly what
Copilot changed side by side.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:56.970
The vulnerability code on the
left, the fix code on the right.

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:58.410
It's not a black box.

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:01.740
I can review it, understand
the reasoning, and accept

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:05.910
or reject each change, just like
I would do in a code review.

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:08.520
This is what developer-first
security looks like.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:11.490
The scanner found it,
AI suggested the fix,

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:12.630
and now I'm reviewing it

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:15.210
in the same editor
that I use every day.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:18.630
No context switching, no
separate security tool,

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:22.290
no ticket that sits in the
backlog for two sprints.

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:23.370
Now, let's look at what happens

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:25.170
when the code hits
a pull request.

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:27.690
The same finding that
the CLI caught locally

00:06:27.690 --> 00:06:29.670
and the pipeline
enforced, it will show

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:32.070
up right here in the PR.

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:33.270
And look at this.

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:37.560
It's not just a red flag saying
"XPath injection found,"

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:39.630
Defender is telling the
developer exactly what's

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:40.620
going wrong.

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:44.040
The header value is injected
directly into the KPath query,

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:45.930
letting an attacker
alter the predicate

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:48.570
and disclose data
they shouldn't see.

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:52.530
And right below the finding,
Copilot's suggested fix.

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:54.420
Parameterize the KPath query,

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:58.410
pass the input as
a bound variable.

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:01.500
Even tells you to
verify the fix works.

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:03.960
This is the developer
security loop in action.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:06.960
The CLI catches it,
the pipeline gates it,

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:09.480
and the PR annotates
it with enough context

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:13.470
to fix it in minutes, not days.

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:14.490
All right.

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:15.810
Now let's pivot.

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:18.180
We've been living in the
developer's world: terminal,

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:21.600
VS Code, GitHub
Actions, pull requests.

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:22.950
Now let's switch hats.

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:26.130
You're a security manager, and
you need to answer one question:

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:30.030
Across all my repos, all my
pipelines, all my teams,

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:33.520
where is my application
security posture?

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:38.850
This is the AI code
security initiative.

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:42.870
Think of it as your team's
single pane of glass

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:45.990
for everything we just saw
on the developer side.

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:50.220
Every finding from every
scan, the CLI, the pipeline,

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:53.910
agentless code scanning,
it all rolls up to here.

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:57.990
You can see the overall score,
the number of recommendations,

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:00.210
which repositories
have findings,

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:03.120
and how they're trending.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:05.910
There isn't a list of alerts
that you have to go hunt for.

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:07.800
It's all curated,
prioritized view

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:10.560
of your application
security risk.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:13.260
And here's what makes
this most powerful.

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:16.260
These aren't just code
findings sitting in isolation.

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:19.260
Defender is correlating them
with your cloud environment.

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:23.040
That XPath injection we found in
the CLI, if that code is running

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:26.220
in a container that's
internet-exposed with a path

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:29.160
of sensitive data, it's
going to surface here

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:33.480
with a completely different risk
score than the same finding

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:35.860
in an internal dev
tool would have.

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:42.000
Each recommendation, it
traces back to the source,

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:45.060
the repository,
the line of code.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:48.570
The security team sees the
risk, the developer sees

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:52.470
where to fix it, the same data,
but with a different lens.

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:54.300
So let me go a little
bit deeper, even more.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:57.270
Let me show you what an attack
path looks like when one

00:08:57.270 --> 00:09:00.450
of these code vulnerabilities is
actually running in production.

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:03.390
So we just saw initiative,
your security posture

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:05.520
across all your repositories.

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:07.590
But a list of findings
isn't enough.

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:09.000
The real question is, "Which

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:12.300
of these findings actually
puts me at risk right now?"

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:15.310
That's what the
attack path answers.

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:20.970
Defender doesn't just find
vulnerabilities in isolation.

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:24.000
It maps them to your live
cloud environment and asks,

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.340
"Can an attacker
actually reach this?

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:29.110
What can they get if
they exploit it?"

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:32.250
If I take a look at one
of these vulnerabilities,

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:35.850
I can actually filter
on the risk factors,

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:42.210
such as being internet-exposed,
and having sensitive data path.

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:46.350
This is really where we can
start prioritizing and filtering

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:49.920
on what we consider to be the
riskiest applications running

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:52.080
in real time.

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:57.060
So to me, those things
combined -- internet exposure,

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:02.010
a path to something valuable,
a known vulnerability.

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:06.090
Any one of those alone might be
a little priority, but together,

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:08.580
that's your biggest risk.

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:12.180
I'm going to take a
look at an attack path.

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:13.650
I know that this
one is in Azure.

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:16.680
I'm going to look at this one
in the Azure portal itself.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:19.590
So here you can see that this
attack path, as I mentioned,

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:21.780
has internet exposure.

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:25.710
It has high-severity
vulnerabilities.

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:30.180
I'm going to take a look at
these vulnerabilities itself.

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:31.680
With this recommendation view,

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:34.530
I can look at all the
known vulnerabilities

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:36.400
for this container.

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:43.140
This package actually was in
the news relatively recently.

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:44.280
And I'm going to take a look

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:49.830
to see the actual
code-to-runtime phases.

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:53.130
To me, this is a
very powerful view.

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:59.130
So, in this view, I can see,
going from code, the repository

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:00.480
that the source lies in.

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:02.940
Build, the pipeline
that built it.

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:07.890
Ship, the registry that
contains the container.

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:11.280
And runtime, the actual cluster
that's actually running

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:12.840
this container.

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:15.600
I can see the actual
source that built it,

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:17.970
and this is very powerful
because I can look into here

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:20.460
and I can look at the
source vulnerabilities

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:22.290
that were identified
in this one,

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:24.720
and I can start prioritizing
this one even higher

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:27.390
because I know that this
container that's running this

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:32.700
application, it has these
vulnerabilities inside running

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:35.970
in an internet-exposed and
accessing sensitive data.

00:11:35.970 --> 00:11:38.280
So you can see here, I
have code injections.

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:41.670
I can start prioritizing
these code injections knowing

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:44.080
that this is a critical asset.

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:49.710
Even better, one of the
biggest issues and problems

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:55.470
that security teams have is
tracing back to exactly where

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:59.280
or who needs to fix the
vulnerability itself, right?

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:00.540
They can put it into some kind

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:04.170
of tracking board
or issue board.

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:07.380
It might get lost
in the backlog.

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:09.690
They can take a lot of time to
actually find the developer

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:11.790
and find the repository.

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:14.970
But with our GitHub Advanced
Security integration,

00:12:14.970 --> 00:12:17.430
it allows you to take
action immediately.

00:12:17.430 --> 00:12:18.600
And with this action,

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:24.480
I can create an issue
directly from this view.

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:25.830
This one has already
been created,

00:12:25.830 --> 00:12:27.300
so I'm actually just
going to take a look

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:30.180
at it right now inside GitHub
on what it looks like.

00:12:30.180 --> 00:12:33.180
So it creates an
issue in GitHub.

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:35.700
But it does more than
just create that issue.

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:39.030
It actually will
assign it to Copilot.

00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:42.900
You can see that if
I fix this package,

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:46.650
I will actually fix three
additional CVEs as well.

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:49.050
So not only that one
critical we found,

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:52.920
but there's two additional
ones other than that.

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:56.880
It will go in and identify the
exact place and the exact code

00:12:56.880 --> 00:12:58.350
where it needs to be fixed.

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:02.580
What used to take developers
hours to identify,

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:05.310
Copilot will find
it immediately.

00:13:05.310 --> 00:13:06.990
In this case, it's
just to update

00:13:06.990 --> 00:13:10.590
and package it
from 0.21 to 1.13.

00:13:10.590 --> 00:13:13.860
But it could potentially be
buried in your base images

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:19.020
or other packages that have
a more transitive property.

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:22.290
So going back here, this is
our GitHub Advanced Security

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:26.700
integration with Defender
Cloud, where you can --

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:29.550
where security teams can
meet developers exactly

00:13:29.550 --> 00:13:30.780
where they work at.

00:13:30.780 --> 00:13:33.150
Let's go back to the
cloud inventory and talk

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:35.070
about something most security
teams are even thinking

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:36.150
about yet.

00:13:36.150 --> 00:13:40.320
AI models are flowing into your
environment every single day,

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:43.620
from Hugging Face, from
internal training pipelines,

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:45.540
from shared registries.

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:48.690
How many of them actually
have been inspected?

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:50.400
This isn't hypothetical.

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:51.570
Earlier this year, attackers

00:13:51.570 --> 00:13:54.540
on Hugging Face were
weaponizing model artifacts,

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:56.340
using pickle serializations

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:59.220
to embed malicious payloads
inside what looked

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:01.320
like a normal PyTorch model.

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:03.270
You download it, you load it,

00:14:03.270 --> 00:14:05.730
and the moment pickle
deserializes that file,

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:08.010
it executes arbitrary code.

00:14:08.010 --> 00:14:10.980
And even though the industry is
pushing towards safer formats

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:15.990
like TensorFlow and SafeTensors,
we still see a massive amount

00:14:15.990 --> 00:14:19.260
of PyTorch in production.

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:23.520
This is what Defender gives
you: discoverability first.

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:26.130
Customers I talk to are
genuinely surprised.

00:14:26.130 --> 00:14:28.860
They didn't know how many
models were actually running

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:31.050
across their workspace
and registries.

00:14:31.050 --> 00:14:33.150
There's no central
location by default.

00:14:33.150 --> 00:14:37.890
You have models across
multiple Azure ML workspaces,

00:14:37.890 --> 00:14:39.570
across multiple registries.

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:43.080
Defender finds all of them and
tells you exactly what you have.

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:45.510
You see these models
with the yellow bar?

00:14:45.510 --> 00:14:47.520
That means Defender
found something.

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:50.010
Right now, we're populating
recommendations specifically

00:14:50.010 --> 00:14:53.400
on models that contain malicious
or vulnerable content.

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:56.460
So if you see a recommendation
here, pay attention.

00:14:56.460 --> 00:14:59.260
It means the scanner
found something real.

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:06.150
Here's the model properties:
version, type, criticality.

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:07.650
And let me go into
the asset page

00:15:07.650 --> 00:15:09.540
to find even more information.

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:13.020
You can see the security
recommendation Defender has

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:14.490
actually pushed.

00:15:14.490 --> 00:15:19.080
And if I go into the threats
and the vulnerabilities,

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:21.960
I can now see -- there it is.

00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:22.590
Right there.

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:25.110
I can see that the scanner found

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:28.320
that this specific pickle
file contains a malicious

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:29.940
serialization payload.

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:32.670
This is the same type of
attack we just talked about,

00:15:32.670 --> 00:15:35.340
except now, it's sitting in
your production environment,

00:15:35.340 --> 00:15:39.120
potentially connected
to a live endpoint.

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:43.050
So the remediation is pretty
straightforward: disable

00:15:43.050 --> 00:15:46.740
or unpublish the affected
model version in the registry

00:15:46.740 --> 00:15:50.790
or workspace, archive it,
disconnect it from the endpoint,

00:15:50.790 --> 00:15:52.830
then deploy it with
a clean version.

00:15:52.830 --> 00:15:56.250
It's the exact same principle
as a container image.

00:15:56.250 --> 00:15:58.290
It's an immutable artifact.

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:01.320
Replace, don't patch.

00:16:01.320 --> 00:16:04.410
But what if you can catch this
before it's ever deployed?

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:07.050
That same CLI scanner
we showed earlier,

00:16:07.050 --> 00:16:09.600
it can scan model
artifacts, too.

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:10.830
So we just saw what happens

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:13.170
when a malicious model is
already in your environment.

00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:14.370
Now, let me show you
how it stops them

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:16.290
from getting there
in the first place.

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:18.180
I've got a different repo here.

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:20.340
This one has machine
learning training scripts,

00:16:20.340 --> 00:16:24.600
model artifacts, and a
deployment pipeline.

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:28.030
Now, watch what happens
when this pipeline ran.

00:16:32.940 --> 00:16:34.890
You can see that it
found low-severity

00:16:34.890 --> 00:16:36.510
vulnerabilities here.

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:39.540
Meaning, if we do find any
kind of vulnerability,

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:42.900
you should consider
it to be very risky.

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:45.480
Looking into the
pipeline itself,

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:53.340
you can see us scanning
using the Defender CLI,

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:56.730
and the pipeline actually
catches it before it actually

00:16:56.730 --> 00:16:59.190
finishes here.

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:01.860
This is end-to-end AI security.

00:17:01.860 --> 00:17:05.280
Discover what you have,
scan it in the pipeline,

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:07.680
flag what's dangerous
in production,

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:14.160
and give you a clear path to fix
it, from code to deployed model.

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:15.900
So let's bring
this all together.

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:17.880
You saw a complete
security lifecycle,

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:19.740
from the very first line of code

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:22.500
to a running workload
in the cloud.

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:25.650
A developer scanned
locally with the CLI --

00:17:25.650 --> 00:17:32.010
-- got AI-powered fixes, and
reviewed them all in VS Code.

00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:36.720
The same engine ran in GitHub
Actions pipeline as a gate.

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:39.150
The PR got annotated
with findings,

00:17:39.150 --> 00:17:41.280
and Copilot suggested fixes.

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:45.600
The security team saw everything
roll up to an initiative view,

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:48.450
discovered attack paths that
connected code vulnerabilities

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:52.650
to real code risk, and traced a
running container all the way

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:56.130
back to a commit that
introduced the problem.

00:17:56.130 --> 00:17:59.070
A GitHub issue was created,
Copilot wrote the fix,

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:00.620
and the loop was closed.

00:18:00.620 --> 00:18:03.200
And then we went even further
into AI model security,

00:18:03.200 --> 00:18:05.540
catching a malicious pickle
file in the pipeline

00:18:05.540 --> 00:18:07.250
and managing the AI
security posture

00:18:07.250 --> 00:18:09.380
across your entire
model inventory.

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:10.560
Thanks for watching.

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:11.460
Go try it.

00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:13.500
The Defender CLI is
available today,

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:16.620
and everything you just saw is
built into Microsoft Defender

00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:18.910
and GitHub Advanced Security.
